00:29:14,860 --> 00:29:19,740
of the CFTC and the idea that crypto crosses borders, it's important to note

490
00:29:19,740 --> 00:29:22,700
that crypto is a global thing.

491
00:29:22,700 --> 00:29:24,020
It's not domestic.

492
00:29:24,020 --> 00:29:25,940
It doesn't recognize borders.

493
00:29:25,940 --> 00:29:30,260
And so people like us and institutions that are going to play a role in the

494
00:29:30,260 --> 00:29:34,300
space need to think about what it means to be regulated globally.

495
00:29:34,300 --> 00:29:37,660
This comes, I think, fairly natural to folks that were in the FX industry,

496
00:29:37,660 --> 00:29:41,740
because if you're in FX, you have to have a relationship with the JFSA in Japan

497
00:29:41,740 --> 00:29:46,740
and ASIC in Australia and the FCA in the UK and the US regulators.

498
00:29:46,740 --> 00:29:52,140
But this is not a trivial thing because the way that Mika frameworks works may not

499
00:29:52,140 --> 00:29:55,860
be identical to what happens in Singapore or in Japan or in the US.

500
00:29:55,860 --> 00:30:00,300
And so I think that's the big topic is for people that are going to compete,

501
00:30:00,300 --> 00:30:02,460
they've got to compete globally and they've got to think about what that

502
00:30:02,460 --> 00:30:04,740
actually means to their business.

503
00:30:04,740 --> 00:30:08,860
I have something much more nuanced, though, for you that we can kind of kick around.

504
00:30:08,860 --> 00:30:12,740
I think there is a potential for one big shocker in the crypto space from a

505
00:30:12,740 --> 00:30:18,100
regulatory standpoint, and that is, in my experience, when you look at the growth

506
00:30:18,100 --> 00:30:23,220
of retail markets, be it in equities or be it in foreign exchange,

507
00:30:23,220 --> 00:30:28,380
normally what happens is there's some practice related to execution that's not

508
00:30:28,380 --> 00:30:33,580
quite right and the regulator comes in and figures it out and then it finds all

509
00:30:33,580 --> 00:30:37,900
the companies and then they stop that behavior and then onward you go.

510
00:30:37,900 --> 00:30:40,420
In retail effects, the idea was positive slippage.

511
00:30:40,420 --> 00:30:44,540
So all of the brokers on stop orders, clients would get negatively slipped.

512
00:30:44,540 --> 00:30:47,460
That's fine. But on a limit order, if there was a positive slippage,

513
00:30:47,460 --> 00:30:49,220
the broker would keep it.

514
00:30:49,220 --> 00:30:54,940
So when regulations spread in retail effects in kind of the 2005 to 2010 era,

515
00:30:54,940 --> 00:30:56,700
global regulators caught this.

516
00:30:56,700 --> 00:31:00,500
They made examples, two million, three million, four million dollar fines.

517
00:31:00,500 --> 00:31:02,220
And then that practice went away.

518
00:31:02,260 --> 00:31:05,540
I think crypto has one big disease that should be discussed,

519
00:31:05,540 --> 00:31:10,060
which is the exchange model itself, the central limit order book model,

520
00:31:10,060 --> 00:31:13,580
doesn't recognize liquidity providers and retail clients.

521
00:31:13,580 --> 00:31:15,140
There's only customers.

522
00:31:15,140 --> 00:31:18,300
It's everyone swimming in one swimming pool.

523
00:31:18,300 --> 00:31:23,380
And so what happens is you have all these HFTs and market makers that are now in

524
00:31:23,380 --> 00:31:24,700
this pool.

525
00:31:24,700 --> 00:31:31,300
And so the question becomes, if Bitcoin crashes 40% right now as we're on this,

526
00:31:31,300 --> 00:31:33,060
who's getting out of their risk first?

527
00:31:33,060 --> 00:31:37,220
Is it the institution or is it the retail client?

528
00:31:37,220 --> 00:31:39,180
I'm pretty sure I know the answer.

529
00:31:39,180 --> 00:31:42,700
And I'm pretty sure it's really bad for the retail client.

530
00:31:42,700 --> 00:31:44,580
I'm sure if they want to exit their position,

531
00:31:44,580 --> 00:31:48,060
they're going to get out at exponentially worse prices because of this.

532
00:31:48,060 --> 00:31:52,620
And to me, this is an inherent conflict of interest that would be deemed

533
00:31:52,620 --> 00:31:54,900
unacceptable in any other market.

534
00:31:54,900 --> 00:31:56,700
I think it's going to take a while,

535
00:31:56,700 --> 00:32:00,780
but I do think the Western regulators are going to dig into that.

536
00:32:00,780 --> 00:32:04,980
I don't think that that practice is something that we should bank on moving

537
00:32:04,980 --> 00:32:05,940
forward.

538
00:32:05,940 --> 00:32:09,620
And just the way that these native crypto exchanges have built themselves,

539
00:32:09,620 --> 00:32:12,900
I think that that could be very, very problematic.

540
00:32:12,900 --> 00:32:16,820
Would you classify that as a best execution standard for crypto?

541
00:32:16,820 --> 00:32:20,220
How would we actually talk about that once that comes to pass?

542
00:32:20,220 --> 00:32:23,780
I absolutely think it fits within the best execution framework.

543
00:32:23,780 --> 00:32:26,940
And notably, it's around the concept of a conflict of interest.

544
00:32:26,940 --> 00:32:29,660
Regulators, as we know, we saw it in the Mika framework.

545
00:32:29,660 --> 00:32:31,540
I would expect it here in the US.

546
00:32:31,540 --> 00:32:36,500
Normally, the first driver of their focus is aimed at the protection of retail

547
00:32:36,500 --> 00:32:37,500
consumers.

548
00:32:37,500 --> 00:32:39,940
This is a practice that I think is fairly obvious.

549
00:32:39,940 --> 00:32:41,740
It's not to their interest.

550
00:32:41,740 --> 00:32:43,740
And it's not something we see in other markets.

551
00:32:43,740 --> 00:32:46,940
When market makers price sorrel schwa for equities,

552
00:32:46,940 --> 00:32:49,780
you have liquidity on one side and retail on the other.

553
00:32:49,780 --> 00:32:51,820
In retail foreign exchange, it's the same way.

554
00:32:51,820 --> 00:32:55,300
You have liquidity on one side and you have clients on the other.

555
00:32:55,300 --> 00:32:58,980
And there's numerous retail brokers in the crypto space

556
00:32:58,980 --> 00:33:01,060
that do operate that way.

557
00:33:01,060 --> 00:33:05,340
And those are normally traditional online brokers who have come into

558
00:33:05,340 --> 00:33:08,180
spot crypto. And so they've got liquidity on one side of their tech and

559
00:33:08,180 --> 00:33:09,500
retail on the other.

560
00:33:09,500 --> 00:33:11,340
There is precedent for doing it that way.

561
00:33:11,340 --> 00:33:14,660
But certainly, this would fall under best execution and an inherent

562
00:33:14,660 --> 00:33:16,020
conflict of interest.

563
00:33:16,020 --> 00:33:17,740
It'll be interesting to see how that evolves.

564
00:33:17,740 --> 00:33:19,780
And it's not something anyone's talking about right now.

565
00:33:19,780 --> 00:33:21,620
But if this does start to institutionalize,

566
00:33:21,620 --> 00:33:25,420
there's no reason to believe that all of the concepts that exist in other asset

567
00:33:25,420 --> 00:33:28,140
classes wouldn't just be brought to bear here.

568
00:33:28,140 --> 00:33:32,740
That's right. Sometimes I get overly philosophical so I can spend hours.

569
00:33:32,740 --> 00:33:33,900
It's not nefarious.

570
00:33:33,900 --> 00:33:38,100
It's not something that these companies dreamt up to create a conflict of

571
00:33:38,100 --> 00:33:39,460
interest at all.

572
00:33:39,460 --> 00:33:43,060
But I do think it underscores that when you see a lot of the founders of the

573
00:33:43,060 --> 00:33:45,180
retail crypto exchanges speak,

574
00:33:45,180 --> 00:33:48,060
they don't see themselves as a financial broker.

575
00:33:48,060 --> 00:33:50,500
They don't see themselves as a Charles Schwab.

576
00:33:50,500 --> 00:33:54,100
They see themselves as almost like an Expedia.com.

577
00:33:54,100 --> 00:33:55,340
When they use the word exchange,

578
00:33:55,340 --> 00:33:58,140
they're talking about it almost in those types of terms,

579
00:33:58,140 --> 00:34:03,100
not in exchange the way the CME would talk about themselves as an exchange.

580
00:34:03,100 --> 00:34:07,900
I think for them, this would be alarming on one hand and I think very surprising.

581
00:34:07,900 --> 00:34:11,460
I don't think it's something that people have really thought through all that

582
00:34:11,460 --> 00:34:15,260
much. I think they just think that the way that this thing works operationally

583
00:34:15,260 --> 00:34:17,060
is totally fine.

584
00:34:17,060 --> 00:34:20,380
Better to get out in front of stuff like this so that the next version of

585
00:34:20,380 --> 00:34:24,020
Gary Gensler doesn't get his hands on it and make everyone's life very difficult

586
00:34:24,420 --> 00:34:26,300
in four or eight years, however long.

587
00:34:26,300 --> 00:34:29,700
We'd love to close it off with just a little bit of a refresher on crossover.

